Data structures and databases both organize information, but they operate at different levels of the system hierarchy.
A Data Structure manages temporary data in volatile main memory (RAM) while a program runs, whereas a Database manages persistent data stored on non-volatile media (SSD/Disk) across multiple application sessions and systems.
Under the hood, databases rely heavily on data structures (like B-Trees, Hash Tables, and LSM-Trees) to organize their storage files on disk efficiently.
